The Gregorian calendar, which is widely used today, divides all 12 months into two categories based on the number of days they contain. Seven months have 31 days, four months have 30 days, and one month, February, has 28 or 29 days in a leap year.

The number of days in each month was determined by historical and astronomical factors. The Roman calendar originally had 10 months, but Augustus Caesar renamed one month from September to October after his own victory over Octavian, adding an extra day. Over time, the varying lengths of the months were standardized, with February being designated to have fewer days because it was considered an unlucky month.

The reason February does not follow the pattern of having 31 days is rooted in this rich history and the specific designations made by ancient Roman leaders and astronomers. Therefore, the current structure of the calendar is maintained despite seeming unusual compared to other months.

